/** @page dev-docs Documentation

VidRe developers are required to **generously document the code**. This means 
three main things, regardless of the programming language:

1. Important steps in the program flow are documented.

    This is especially helpful for readers when bit-twiddling, recursion, metaprogramming, 
    or other non‑obvious techniques are used.

    Language-specific examples are provided in the respective sections below.

2. Essential API elements must be documented.

    Enumerations, classes, methods, functions, modules, etc. that are part of 
    the exposed API must have proper documentation. Exceptions apply only for 
    trivial or purely internal items.

3. The API structure is reflected by file/directory structure (and vice versa).

    A developer should be able to guess where to find a piece of functionality 
    based on the API name alone. Functionality that is independently 
    categorizable usually also uses distinct interface or implementation files.

    @see @ref dev-layout

## C++

For C++ documentation VidRe uses [doxygen](https://doxygen.nl). Contributors
should observe previous usage within the source-code tree.

<b>Examples</b>:<br/>
See `@addtogroup` and `@defgroup` in 
[vidre/core/video.hh](https://github.com/bbo-lab/vidre/blob/main/src/libs/video/vidre/video.hh) and
[api.dox](https://github.com/bbo-lab/vidre/blob/main/src/libs/api.dox)

## Lua

@TBD{
At the moment of writing, Lua is not yet actively used in the project. The are
projects that allow including Lua into the doxygen world 
(see [Luals2dox](https://github.com/AndreasMatthias/Luals2dox)),
therefore, it is recommended to use doxygen-style documentation in Lua files.
}

## Python

@TBD{At the moment of writing, native Python integration is only a planned feature.}

*/
